Thanks to Dr Tom Blinn for his input to discussions surrounding this
problem.

I eventually pointed the finger at Oracle, after doing a cut and paste job I
highlighted that it was the connection to Oracle that caused the signal to
be hidden. I raised this with Oracle and they promptly provided the
workaround.

The Oracle response is attached. The problem appeared in Oracle 8.0.5 and
relates only to the use of the SIGCHLD signal.

Regards,

Mat.


 <<Oracle TAR Solution.txt>>  <<Oracle TAR Additional.txt>> 

> At the risk of going off topic...
> 
> We have just upgraded from T64 4.0D to T64 5.1A. A bespoke system that we
> run is now behaving strangely. We have a daemon process that spawns other
> processes and then when these complete does some housekeeping. The
> housekeeping is initiated by the SIGCHLD signal. Now that we have upgraded
> to 5.1A the handler no longer seems to function correctly.
> 
> Is anyone aware of any issues with 5.1A in this area?
> 
> If there are any budding C/C++ programmers out there I can provide further
> info on the function calls that are used by this daemon process.
> 
> Thanks for your time.
> 
> Mat.
