Even better said:

Apache 1.3.x is a proven stable code base. Apache 2.0
adds tons of new 
features. Finally the web server is threaded, modules
can be chained 
together for processing request, and other very
interesting things. 
When 
2.0 started much of the stable code base was
rewritten. Many like the 
idea of using a proven package in the 1.3.x line while
2.0 continues to 
mature. This is why many people are continuing to use
1.3.x

For instance the plugin architecture of 2.0 is
somewhat different than 
1.3.x, so when compiling mod_myfavoriteapachemodule,
it probably won't 
work for 2.0 without a significant rewrite. Last I
checked php was only 
starting to be supported and I think it is still
considered ALPHA for 
apache 2.0. There are dozens if not hundreds of
modules for apache 1.3 
that work well, some of which are long abandoned by
thier original 
programmers. They work in 1.3 and may or may not get
ported to 2.0

I can't make many recommendations about which, but I
hope I've given 
you 
some background into the differences.

--
Jay R. Wren
Systems Programmer
Oakland University


The 2 most significant differences, in my opinion, are
in threading and
memory management.  The threading improves scalability
(usually) on 
systems
with POSIX thread support.  Nearly every module in
Apache 2.0 can be 
used as
a DSO.

Elizabeth Harvey-Forsythe
MIT Media Lab


